A||||18||Oreif||Sail Panel Speaker Upgrade Pics.||||14||
Z||000000||Oreif||02-08-2001||02:45 PM||||Here are pics of my sail panel speakers prior to install. They are 4" components. The crossover will be mounted inside the frame. How do they look?<P>Quick question, Anyone have an idea on how to clean the grill cloth on the sail panel covers? They are "melted" onto the sail panel so replacing the cloth looks difficult to remount the cover.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Speaker_set.jpg">  <P><BR>Thanks,<P><BR>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-08-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000001||bHooper||02-08-2001||03:02 PM||||nice idea, to locate your tweeters in that location.<P>as for cleaning the cloth... i am very pleased with the use of the blue 409 to clean things over the years.  however you could also try to use some liquid dishwashing detergent, or laundry detergent, warm water, and a cloth.  gently scrubbing the area and spray it off with warm water.<P>as for the rest of the plastic panels... i have recently discovered that soft scrub does an amazing job on these things!  after scrubbing, wipe off with a warm wet cloth, let dry, then wipe off with 409 to remove the haze.<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||02-08-2001||03:06 PM||||THANKS! I'll give that a try.||||reg||14
Z||000003||Haze_Performance||02-08-2001||06:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bHooper:<BR><B>then wipe off with 409 to remove the haze.<P>hoop</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Did someone call me???  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> <BR>----------------------------------<P>Looking good Oreif..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ttttttt.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>WS6 Fieros "Members Photo Page"</A>||||reg||11
Z||000004||theogre||02-08-2001||07:25 PM||||try mixing some laundry soap with water. Just a spoon full or 2. let it sit and soak awhile. just shake the cover in the water. avoid scrubbing it if you can.<P>If you can't get them off the pannel, put the soap&water in a spray bottle. keep them wet awhile. then just rinse them. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||bHooper||02-08-2001||07:33 PM||||too funny, i didn't think of that when i was typeing... <I>haze</I><P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000006||PearlBlueSoul||02-08-2001||08:58 PM||||Umm, suggestion on what crossovers to use:  <P>Depending on what you've got (I duno what setup you're running) if you're not running an amp with an adjustable crossover, and you're geting in-line crossovers, get 0-300Hz ones.  I've tried 600 HZ and 200 HZ.  100 Hz under is not enough, and 600 is way too much.  cuts out much of the mids.  I'd try going for the 300hz ones.  seems to me those would work best.<P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||02-08-2001||11:42 PM||||As for the rest of the system, I am using the Polk Crossovers between the mids and tweeters. As for crossing from the sub to the Polks, I am using a seperate electronic crossover modified by me. It'll cross at 110hz at 24db roll off. The Polks will get 50W each set but the rears will be delayed using a digital processor. I haven't figured out the delay yet but it is adjustable. I figure I could play with the delay until I achieve the correct front stage/rear fill level. I will also be able to tweek in the imaging of left and right with the processor.<BR>If everything goes as planned I should have excellent staging and seperation. I am setting it up for acoustical accuracy rather than the "can kill small animals within 5 feet" style. With an analyzer I should be well within the 3db curve. I am hoping to actually get down to less than 1.5db slope. I just don't know what the resonance frequency of the interior will be. At a guess it should be somewhere around 125-135hz due to the interior volume of the Fiero. As to how much needs to be toned out, I won't know that until I power the system up. Clean, clear, and tuned sounds a whole lot better than the kid with the beater car with 4 15" subs in the back and 2 tweeters in front all driven by a 1000W amp.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||sqoach||02-08-2001||11:53 PM||||i now feel like i know nothing about stereos and systems and such. thanks oreif.<P><BR>hehe j/k  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>you sure do know your stuff.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig2.JPG"> ||||reg||11
Z||000009||Oreif||02-09-2001||12:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sqoach:<BR><B>i now feel like i know nothing about stereos and systems and such. thanks oreif.<P><BR>hehe j/k   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>you sure do know your stuff.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>LOL sorry didn't mean to confuse anyone. Also just so everyone knows, I am doing this for my personal preference. I feel that everyone should design their stereo systems to their preferences. I have entered many car stereo contests over the years and have realized that the contests are not worth it.<BR>They have their ideas how my system should sound and if you don't use the recommended equipment (usually the high end brand names) you'll never win. Stereo systems are installed for the drivers listening pleasure not some guy paid by a stereo manufacturer to judge them.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfixit58||02-09-2001||07:24 AM||||Hey Oreif,<P>I cleaned mine with 409 and a water hose... while they were out of the car, of course. Spray with 409, let sit for several minutes then hose off and let dry. They look like new.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000011||RiceCooker||02-09-2001||10:09 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B> Clean, clear, and tuned sounds a whole lot better than the kid with the beater car with 4 15" subs in the back and 2 tweeters in front all driven by a 1000W amp.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I hear you Orief! (pun intended)<p>[This message has been edited by RiceCooker (edited 02-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Dan87GT||02-09-2001||10:13 AM||||For my speaker covers I cleaned them with 409, let them dry and then sprayed them with grey fabric die, about 4 coats. They look like new!||||reg||1
Z||000013||deceler8||02-09-2001||10:38 AM||||My solution...tear the cloth off...I did it and it looks just fine.<P>Also tore the cloth off my front grills...looks even better.<P>I tore the cloth off because mine were ripped and I was going to recover them. Then I discovered they really didn't look too bad without the cloth. And the plastic is paintable, if that's the route you want to go.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Oreif||02-09-2001||11:52 AM||||OK, Thanks guys. I sprayed a little "Resolve" on to the grill cloth Then soaked it with 409. While it was soaking I scrubbed the rest of the panel with "Soft Scrub" And rinsed it all off with warm water.<BR>Once dry I installed the tweeter and coated the panel with "Formula 2001 Super Protectant" The Panels came out great!!!!<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Tcleand.jpg">  <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Cleanedup.jpg"> <P>Of course this has now caused another problem.....<BR>I now have to pull out all the other plastic trim and do the same so they match!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Thanks again!<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Oreif||02-09-2001||04:42 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by deceler8:<BR><B>My solution...tear the cloth off...I did it and it looks just fine.<P>Also tore the cloth off my front grills...looks even better.<P>I tore the cloth off because mine were ripped and I was going to recover them. Then I discovered they really didn't look too bad without the cloth. And the plastic is paintable, if that's the route you want to go.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I prefer the look of the cloth. Besides I had to remove the plastic holes from the front grill covers so that my tweeters went unobstructed. I jyst used grill cloth from Radio Shack. The rears came out fine by just cleaning them.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Oreif||03-21-2001||11:40 AM||||Here is the woofer mounted in the sail location. Note it did require a slight size adjustment so the large magnet would fit.<P>Next is installing all the amps, crossovers, and digital processor!<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sail_4.jpg">  <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 03-21-2001).]||||reg||6
